<TopicPage slug="subjects/medicine/intermediate/infection-and-antibiotics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    You met the immune system's soldiers in the lymphatic-and-immunity chapter: the phagocytes that
    engulf invaders within minutes, the antibodies and T-cells that recognise specific enemies and
    remember them for decades. Infectious-disease medicine is what happens when that system is
    overwhelmed, outmanoeuvred, or simply out of time — and the clinician must join the fight from
    outside, with drugs. The peculiar feature of this branch of medicine is that the enemy
    <em>evolves on clinical timescales</em>: bacteria divide every twenty minutes to an hour, and every
    antibiotic course is a selection experiment. So the discipline has two intertwined halves —
    <strong>know your enemy</strong> (which pathogen, which machinery it depends on, which drug
    disables that machinery without disabling you) and <strong>protect your weapons</strong> (because
    careless use breeds resistance, the prescriber's pen is itself an ecological force). The antibiotic
    era, barely eighty years old, turned once-fatal infections into inconveniences; the resistance era
    is the bill coming due.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the enemy taxonomy, the weapons, and the resistance problem" widget="equationStep" config={infectCfg} />

  <h2>Know your enemy: the pathogen families</h2>
  <p>
    The taxonomy matters because it decides the weapon. <strong>Bacteria</strong> are self-contained
    cells with their own machinery — cell wall, ribosomes, DNA — every part of which is a potential
    drug target. They are sorted first by the <strong>Gram stain</strong>: Gram-positive species
    (Staphylococcus, Streptococcus) have thick peptidoglycan walls that hold the purple dye; Gram-negative
    species (E. coli, Klebsiella, Neisseria) have thin peptidoglycan plus an outer membrane that makes
    them intrinsically harder to penetrate, so the stain's colour predicts which drugs can even get in.
    Bacteria are further sorted by shape (cocci, rods) and by their oxygen needs, and all of it feeds
    the empiric question a doctor faces within the hour: "likely Gram-positive cocci from a skin
    source — which drug covers those until the culture returns?" <strong>Viruses</strong> are a
    different category of problem: not cells but genetic payloads in protein coats, with no metabolism
    of their own — they hijack yours. That is why <strong>antibiotics are useless against viruses</strong>,
    a biochemical fact, not a guideline technicality: there is no bacterial wall, ribosome or folate
    pathway to attack. Antivirals must instead target viral enzymes with minimal human counterparts
    (acyclovir on viral DNA polymerase, oseltamivir on influenza neuraminidase) or block entry and
    release. <strong>Fungi</strong> are eukaryotes like us, so drugs must aim at the differences —
    their cell walls and ergosterol membranes (amphotericin, azoles) — which is also why fungal
    infections are the hardest to treat selectively. <strong>Parasites</strong> (malaria, worms,
    protozoa) bring their own life cycles, often with stages in different tissues that different drugs
    hit. In practice, the first treatment is usually <strong>empiric</strong> — a best-guess drug
    against the most likely organisms — then narrowed ("de-escalated") once cultures and sensitivities
    return.
  </p>

  <h2>What infection does: the battle and its collateral</h2>
  <p>
    Much of what "feeling ill" means is not the pathogen's work but your immune system's. Fever is a
    regulated hypothalamic set-point shift driven by pyrogens — many pathogens replicate less well at
    higher temperature, and immune cells work better. Inflammation — redness, heat, swelling, pain —
    is vascular plumbing that delivers phagocytes to the site, and pus is that battle's aftermath:
    dead neutrophils, bacteria and tissue fluid. Most of the time innate immunity wins within days and
    the adaptive system finishes and remembers. The catastrophic failure mode is <strong>sepsis</strong>:
    the response itself becomes dysregulated and systemic — widespread vasodilation drops blood
    pressure, capillaries leak, clotting and inflammation cascade organ-by-organ into failure. Sepsis
    kills more people each year than the infections it complicates, which is why "time is tissue" in
    emergency medicine: antibiotics and fluids within the first hour measurably change survival.
    The clinical lesson generalises: in infection medicine, the treatment always has two targets —
    the pathogen and the host's own response.
  </p>

  <h2>Antibiotics: selective toxicity</h2>
  <p>
    The entire therapeutic trick of an antibiotic is <strong>selective toxicity</strong> — exploiting
    a difference between bacterial and human cells strong enough that you can hurt one and spare the
    other. The classic classes are organised around exactly that principle. <strong>Cell-wall
    synthesis</strong> is the oldest target and the safest, because human cells have no wall: penicillin
    and its descendants (the beta-lactams) and vancomycin prevent the peptidoglycan cross-links from
    forming, so the bacterium literally bursts under its own osmotic pressure. <strong>Protein
    synthesis</strong> targets the bacterial ribosome, which differs from ours enough for drugs to
    discriminate: aminoglycosides, tetracyclines and macrolides bind the 30S or 50S subunits and freeze
    translation. <strong>DNA replication and repair</strong> is hit by fluoroquinolones (bacterial DNA
    gyrase) and metronidazole; <strong>folate synthesis</strong> — a pathway humans simply don't have,
    since we eat folate — by sulphonamides and trimethoprim; <strong>RNA polymerase</strong> by
    rifampicin. Two further axes organise choice: <em>bactericidal</em> drugs kill (penicillins,
    aminoglycosides — preferred when the host's immune system is weak, as in endocarditis) versus
    <em>bacteriostatic</em> drugs that halt growth and let immunity finish the job (tetracyclines,
    macrolides); and <em>spectrum</em> — narrow-spectrum drugs hit few species, broad-spectrum many.
    The story's founding scene is worth knowing precisely: Alexander Fleming returned from holiday in
    September 1928 to find a mould (Penicillium) contaminating a staphylococcus plate — with a clear
    halo of killed bacteria around it. Penicillin was mass-produced in 1943–44, and the drop in
    Allied deaths from wound infection transformed medicine's relationship with infection overnight.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> antibiotics are not "for infections" generically, and they do not
  make you recover faster from colds or flu. Prescribing them for viral illness buys zero benefit and
  pays real costs — side effects, disruption of your protective microbiome, and selection pressure on
  the commensal bacteria that are resistance-gene reservoirs.</p>

  <h2>Resistance: evolution you can watch</h2>
  <p>
    Resistance is natural selection running on clinical timescales, and its mechanics are concrete.
    Bacteria defend themselves four main ways: <strong>enzymatic destruction</strong> of the drug
    (beta-lactamase enzymes cleave penicillins — the reason clavulanic acid is added to
    amoxicillin as a decoy inhibitor), <strong>efflux pumps</strong> that eject the drug faster than it
    enters, <strong>target modification</strong> that quietly changes the lock the drug fits
    (methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus — MRSA — carries an altered enzyme, PBP2a, that
    beta-lactams can no longer bind), and <strong>impermeability</strong> — reduced porin channels,
    or the biofilm matrix that shields whole communities. The genuinely alarming part is
    <strong>horizontal gene transfer</strong>: bacteria swap plasmids — small rings of DNA carrying
    resistance genes — across species and even genera, so a resistance gene born in a gut bacterium
    can arrive in a pathogen within days. Resistance therefore spreads sideways, not just down
    lineages, and every exposure to an antibiotic — in a patient, a hospital, a feedlot — selects for
    the carriers. The consequences are measured, not hypothetical: MRSA is endemic in hospitals
    worldwide; multi-drug-resistant tuberculosis requires two years of toxic therapy; carbapenem-resistant
    Enterobacteriaceae leave last-line drugs only. The O'Neill review (2016) projected that if trends
    held, drug-resistant infections would kill <strong>10 million people a year by 2050</strong> —
    more than cancer — at a cumulative cost near 100 trillion dollars.
  </p>

  <h2>Stewardship: protecting the weapons</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Antimicrobial stewardship</strong> is the organised response: treat only bacterial
    infections (rapid diagnostics to shorten the guessing), choose the narrowest-spectrum drug that
    covers the likely organism, dose correctly (under-dosing selects hardest, because it leaves
    partially-resistant survivors), de-escalate when cultures return, and stop when the course is
    done. The agriculture dimension is the part most people never see: by weight, the majority of the
    world's antibiotic tonnage is used in livestock — in the United States, roughly two-thirds of
    annual sales — historically as growth promoters and prophylaxis in crowded herds, the exact
    conditions that maximise selection pressure. Bans on growth-promotion use (EU 2006, and
    tightening US rules) are the reason that fact is now a policy battleground. Stewardship is
    genuinely a one-world problem: resistance genes travel in food, water and people, so a farm's
    practice in one hemisphere is a hospital's problem in another.
  </p>

  <h2>Vaccines: training before the fight</h2>
  <p>
    Drugs treat infection; vaccines prevent it by pre-training adaptive immunity — presenting the
    immune system with a harmless stand-in for the pathogen so that memory B- and T-cells stand ready.
    The classical platforms each embody a design trade-off: <strong>live attenuated</strong> vaccines
    (measles, oral polio, BCG) replicate mildly and give the strongest, longest memory, at the price of
    caution in immunocompromised patients; <strong>inactivated</strong> and <strong>subunit</strong>
    vaccines (flu shots, hepatitis B surface antigen, acellular pertussis) are safer but weaker, so
    they need adjuvants and boosters; <strong>toxoids</strong> (tetanus, diphtheria) train antibodies
    against the toxin rather than the bug; and <strong>mRNA</strong> and viral-vector vaccines (the
    COVID-19 platforms) deliver just the antigen's blueprint, collapsing development timelines from
    years to months because the platform is reusable — change the sequence, change the target. The
    population-level payoff — how vaccinating a fraction of people protects the unvaccinated via herd
    immunity, and why measles needs roughly 95% coverage — belongs to the epidemiology lesson; here,
    the historical proof of concept: smallpox, killed by global ring vaccination in 1980, remains the
    only human disease ever eradicated, and it was done without a single antiviral drug.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "natural immunity" from catching the disease is not automatically
  superior. It confers memory, all right — but at the price of the disease's full risk: measles before
  vaccination killed about 1–2 per thousand cases and left permanent brain damage in roughly one per
  thousand, risks no modern vaccine approaches. The vaccine's bargain is to buy the immune education
  while skipping the tuition bill.</p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The expert layer starts with the microbiome: humans carry roughly as many bacterial cells as human
    ones (recent estimates put it near 1:1), and this resident flora is not bystander scenery — it
    colonises surfaces pathogens would love, trains the immune system, and synthesises vitamins.
    Broad-spectrum antibiotics dynamite this ecology, which is why C. difficile colitis follows
    clindamycin and cephalosporins: the spore-forming anaerobe waits for its competitors to be cleared,
    then blooms. Faecal microbiota transplantation — restoring the flora wholesale — now cures
    recurrent C. difficile at rates above 80%, one of medicine's strangest and most effective modern
    therapies. Next, <strong>the resistance economics problem</strong>: a new antibiotic is deliberately
    used sparingly (stewardship) and works for a short patent life before resistance erodes it — so the
    market pays least for the drugs society most needs, which is why push-pull funding schemes,
    subscription models (the UK's "Netflix payment" for antibiotics) and public-private partnerships
    now fund pipeline work that pure market logic abandoned. Then the frontier alternatives:
    <strong>phage therapy</strong> — unleashing bacteria-specific viruses, an approach the Soviet
    Union pursued for decades and the West is revisiting case-by-case for untreatable infections;
    <strong>anti-virulence drugs</strong> that disarm rather than kill (blocking toxins or
    quorum-sensing communication), which impose far weaker selection pressure because the bacterium can
    still live; and CRISPR-based systems designed to cut specific resistance genes out of bacterial
    genomes. Finally, keep the frame the chapter opened with: infection medicine is co-evolution
    management. The pathogen's generation time is a million times faster than ours; our only durable
    advantages are depth (the immune system), breadth (the pharmacopoeia), and discipline (stewardship)
    — and the second two are the ones currently under test.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Fleming's penicillin papers; O'Neill, Review on Antimicrobial Resistance (2016); FDA antibiotic sales reports; WHO eradication records; CDC principles of epidemiology.</p>
</TopicPage>
